  P(e|H) = menu weight / menu mass, exact Fractions.  For every
  history H of the exhaustive family and every UNORDERED pair
  {a, b} of distinct menu events at H (each unordered pair IS
  the two ordered pairs (a,b) and (b,a): the tested identity is
  symmetric under the swap), we census:
    - both-orders admissibility (b at Ha AND a at Hb);
    - one-order-refused pairs (the admissibility ASYMMETRY);
    - NEITHER-order-admissible pairs (MUTUAL EXCLUSION);
    - both-orders-admissible but sigma(Hab) != sigma(Hba)
      (non-commuting by the pin's definition; excluded by name);
    - for COMMUTING pairs (the pin's definition: both orders
      admissible AND sigma(Hab) = sigma(Hba), which by (H1)+(H2)
      means the two continuations carry THE SAME ENTIRE FUTURE
      LAW — the generated analogue of [Hab] = [Hba]):
          P(a|H) P(b|Ha)  =?  P(b|H) P(a|Hb)
      recorded FOR BOTH ORDERED DIRECTIONS of the pair.  The
      identity is symmetric under the swap, but the RATIO
      DEFECT d is not (it inverts), so every defect census
      below is over ORDERED pairs: a pair contributing d also
      contributes 1/d.  Nothing then depends on which element
      of the pair the enumeration happens to call 'a'.
    - and, as a STRICTLY STRONGER hypothesis, the sub-census on
      pairs with REFINED RECORD IDENTITY canon(Hab) = canon(Hba)
      (d42b3's committed canonical-DAG functor) — this is paper
      29's literal '[Hab] = [Hba]'.
